    {
      "id": "jbd30KpWY8DAbLy95t05-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 18.263092162866542,
      "y": -271.9069142344466,
      "width": 3122.7476864621844,
      "height": 1365,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1523865035,
      "version": 3524,
      "versionNonce": 1439723083,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790531515685,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Flow:\n\n1) Create/Start a chat\n- User connects to the service and sends a createChat message\n- The service creates a chat record in Chat table and adds entries in ChatParticipants table\n    - In most cases in DynamoDB, these both operations can be done in a single transaction (up to 100 items), but for chats near 100-participant limit we may need to batch the writes\n\n2 types of queries on ChatParticipant table\n- Find all the participants in a chat grp\n    - We can create a composite primary key on chatId in ChatParticipant table\n- Find all the chats a participant is a part of\n    - We can create a GSI (Global Secondary Index - DynamoDB provides this feature) with participantId as the partition key and chatId as the sort key\n\n2) How does send/receive message work?\n- Client sends a message to Chat Server, chat server stores that message in the database, for new message notification, it finds all the participants for that Chat, then maps the Socket connection for each participants and\nsends notification to all\n- For the message notification part to work, we first need to store an in-memory hashmap in Chat Server to keep track of WebSocket connections for each connected users\n- The above point for chat notification is currently working under 2 assumptions that there is only one chat server and all the users that are the part of the chat are online and connected to that server using any of the\nWebSocket connection\n\n3) Users should be able to receive messages sent while they are not online (upto 30 days)\n- Let's keep an \"Inbox\" for each user which will contain all undelivered messages. When messages are sent, we'll write them to the inbox of each recipient user. If they're already online, we can go ahead and try to deliver the\nmessage immediately. If they're not online, we'll store the message and wait for them to come back later.\n- How much write throughput does this add? The vast majority of chats are 1:1, and the average user sends about 20 messages per day. With 200M active users, that's 4B messages/day or roughly 40K messages/second. For\neach message in a 1:1 chat, we write once to Messages and once to Inbox (for the recipient). Even accounting for group chats, we're looking at roughly 100K writes/second — well within DynamoDB's capabilities with userId as\nthe partition key. [See image-1 for more details]\n\n4) User should be able to Send/Receive Media\n\nUpload:\n- When uploading the content, client makes the request to Chat server using a getAttachmentTarget message and asks for the pre-signed URL (TTL) using which it can use to upload media to S3\n- Client will make direct request to that URL to upload the media, and then it will have the S3 URL which it would send to chat server inside the message it was already sending. Chat server can store that URL\n- The users that are going to receive the message, would receive the S3 URL and can download content directly from Blob storage.\n\nNote: This design represents a optimized system\nIn interview, try to go with naive things first,\nList the problems and then go for optimizations\n- Like in this case, for media share, teh naive path is to send media to chat server via socket channel, it stores the media in DB is a separate table. Now the issue is DynamoDB or other databases are not typically build for\nstoring Blob data",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Flow:\n\n1) Create/Start a chat\n- User connects to the service and sends a createChat message\n- The service creates a chat record in Chat table and adds entries in ChatParticipants table\n    - In most cases in DynamoDB, these both operations can be done in a single transaction (up to 100 items), but for chats near 100-participant limit we may need to batch the writes\n\n2 types of queries on ChatParticipant table\n- Find all the participants in a chat grp\n    - We can create a composite primary key on chatId in ChatParticipant table\n- Find all the chats a participant is a part of\n    - We can create a GSI (Global Secondary Index - DynamoDB provides this feature) with participantId as the partition key and chatId as the sort key\n\n2) How does send/receive message work?\n- Client sends a message to Chat Server, chat server stores that message in the database, for new message notification, it finds all the participants for that Chat, then maps the Socket connection for each participants and sends notification to all\n- For the message notification part to work, we first need to store an in-memory hashmap in Chat Server to keep track of WebSocket connections for each connected users\n- The above point for chat notification is currently working under 2 assumptions that there is only one chat server and all the users that are the part of the chat are online and connected to that server using any of the WebSocket connection\n\n3) Users should be able to receive messages sent while they are not online (upto 30 days)\n- Let's keep an \"Inbox\" for each user which will contain all undelivered messages. When messages are sent, we'll write them to the inbox of each recipient user. If they're already online, we can go ahead and try to deliver the message immediately. If they're not online, we'll store the message and wait for them to come back later.\n- How much write throughput does this add? The vast majority of chats are 1:1, and the average user sends about 20 messages per day. With 200M active users, that's 4B messages/day or roughly 40K messages/second. For each message in a 1:1 chat, we write once to Messages and once to Inbox (for the recipient). Even accounting for group chats, we're looking at roughly 100K writes/second — well within DynamoDB's capabilities with userId as the partition key. [See image-1 for more details]\n\n4) User should be able to Send/Receive Media\n\nUpload:\n- When uploading the content, client makes the request to Chat server using a getAttachmentTarget message and asks for the pre-signed URL (TTL) using which it can use to upload media to S3\n- Client will make direct request to that URL to upload the media, and then it will have the S3 URL which it would send to chat server inside the message it was already sending. Chat server can store that URL\n- The users that are going to receive the message, would receive the S3 URL and can download content directly from Blob storage.\n\nNote: This design represents a optimized system\nIn interview, try to go with naive things first,\nList the problems and then go for optimizations\n- Like in this case, for media share, teh naive path is to send media to chat server via socket channel, it stores the media in DB is a separate table. Now the issue is DynamoDB or other databases are not typically build for storing Blob data",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
